⟦P0⟧⟦P1⟧ file reference⟦P2⟧Each Go module is defined by a ⟦P3⟧ file that describes the module's⟦P4⟧properties, including its dependencies on other modules and on versions of Go.⟦P5⟧These properties include:⟦P6⟧The current module's ⟦P7⟧module path⟦P8⟧. This should be a location from which⟦P9⟧the module can be downloaded by Go tools, such as the module code's⟦P10⟧repository location. This serves as a unique identifier, when combined⟦P11⟧with the module's version number. It is also the prefix of the package path for⟦P12⟧all packages in the module. For more about how Go locates the module, see the⟦P13⟧⟦P14⟧Go Modules Reference⟦P15⟧.⟦P16⟧The minimum ⟦P17⟧version of Go⟦P18⟧ required by the current module.⟦P19⟧A list of minimum versions of other ⟦P20⟧modules required⟦P21⟧ by the current module.⟦P22⟧Instructions, optionally, to ⟦P23⟧replace⟦P24⟧ a required module with another⟦P25⟧module version or a local directory, to ⟦P26⟧exclude⟦P27⟧ a specific version of⟦P28⟧a required module, or to ⟦P29⟧ignore⟦P30⟧ specific directories within the module⟦P31⟧when matching package patterns.⟦P32⟧Go generates a ⟦P33⟧ file when you run the ⟦P34⟧⟦P35⟧⟦P36⟧command⟦P37⟧. The following example creates a ⟦P38⟧ file,⟦P39⟧setting the module's module path to ⟦P40⟧:⟦P41⟧Use ⟦P42⟧ commands to manage dependencies. The commands ensure that the⟦P43⟧requirements described in your ⟦P44⟧ file remain consistent and the content of⟦P45⟧your ⟦P46⟧ file is valid. These commands include the ⟦P47⟧⟦P48⟧⟦P49⟧⟦P50⟧and ⟦P51⟧⟦P52⟧⟦P53⟧ and ⟦P54⟧⟦P55⟧⟦P56⟧⟦P57⟧commands.⟦P58⟧For reference on ⟦P59⟧ commands, see ⟦P60⟧Command go⟦P61⟧.⟦P62⟧You can get help from the command line by typing ⟦P63⟧ ⟦P64⟧command-name⟦P65⟧, as⟦P66⟧with ⟦P67⟧.⟦P68⟧⟦P69⟧See also⟦P70⟧⟦P71⟧Go tools make changes to your ⟦P72⟧ file as you use them to manage⟦P73⟧dependencies. For more, see ⟦P74⟧Managing dependencies⟦P75⟧.⟦P76⟧For more details and constraints related to ⟦P77⟧ files, see the ⟦P78⟧Go modules⟦P79⟧reference⟦P80⟧.⟦P81⟧Example ⟦P82⟧⟦P83⟧A ⟦P84⟧ file includes directives as shown in the following example. These are⟦P85⟧described elsewhere in this topic.⟦P86⟧module ⟦P87⟧⟦P88⟧Declares the module's module path, which is the module's unique identifier⟦P89⟧(when combined with the module version number). The module path becomes the⟦P90⟧import prefix for all packages the module contains.⟦P91⟧For more, see ⟦P92⟧⟦P93⟧ directive⟦P94⟧ in the⟦P95⟧Go Modules Reference.⟦P96⟧Syntax ⟦P97⟧⟦P98⟧module-path⟦P99⟧The module's module path, usually the repository location from which⟦P100⟧the module can be downloaded by Go tools. For module versions v2 and⟦P101⟧later, this value must end with the major version number, such as⟦P102⟧⟦P103⟧.⟦P104⟧Examples ⟦P105⟧⟦P106⟧The following examples substitute ⟦P107⟧ for a repository domain from⟦P108⟧which the module could be downloaded.⟦P109⟧Module declaration for a v0 or v1 module:⟦P110⟧Module path for a v2 module:⟦P111⟧Notes ⟦P112⟧⟦P113⟧The module path must uniquely identify your module. For most modules, the path⟦P114⟧is a ⟦P115⟧ where the ⟦P116⟧ command can find the code (or a redirect to the code).⟦P117⟧For modules that won't ever be downloaded directly, the module path⟦P118⟧can be just some name you control that will ensure uniqueness. The prefix⟦P119⟧⟦P120⟧ is also reserved for use in examples like these.⟦P121⟧For more details, see ⟦P122⟧Managing dependencies⟦P123⟧.⟦P124⟧In practice, the module path is typically the module source's repository domain⟦P125⟧and path to the module code within the repository. The ⟦P126⟧ command⟦P127⟧relies on this form when downloading module versions to resolve dependencies⟦P128⟧on the module user's behalf.⟦P129⟧Even if you're not at first intending to make your module available for use⟦P130⟧from other code, using its repository path is a best practice that will help⟦P131⟧you avoid having to rename the module if you publish it later.⟦P132⟧If at first you don't know the module's eventual repository location, consider⟦P133⟧temporarily using a safe substitute, such as the name of a domain you own or⟦P134⟧a name you control (such as your company name), along with a path following⟦P135⟧from the module's name or source directory. For more, see⟦P136⟧⟦P137⟧Managing dependencies⟦P138⟧.⟦P139⟧For example, if you're developing in a ⟦P140⟧ directory, your temporary⟦P141⟧module path might be ⟦P142⟧, as in the following example,⟦P143⟧where ⟦P144⟧company-name⟦P145⟧ is your company's name:⟦P146⟧go ⟦P147⟧⟦P148⟧Indicates that the module was written assuming the semantics of the Go version⟦P149⟧specified by the directive.⟦P150⟧For more, see ⟦P151⟧⟦P152⟧ directive⟦P153⟧ in the⟦P154⟧Go Modules Reference.⟦P155⟧Syntax ⟦P156⟧⟦P157⟧minimum-go-version⟦P158⟧The minimum version of Go required to compile packages in this module.⟦P159⟧Examples ⟦P160⟧⟦P161⟧Module must run on Go version ⟦P162⟧ or later:⟦P163⟧Notes ⟦P164⟧⟦P165⟧The ⟦P166⟧ directive sets the minimum version of Go required to use this module.⟦P167⟧Before Go ⟦P168⟧, the directive was advisory only; now it is a mandatory requirement:⟦P169⟧Go toolchains refuse to use modules declaring newer Go versions.⟦P170⟧The ⟦P171⟧ directive is an input into selecting which Go toolchain to run.⟦P172⟧See “⟦P173⟧Go toolchains⟦P174⟧” for details.⟦P175⟧The ⟦P176⟧ directive affects use of new language features:⟦P177⟧For packages within the module, the compiler rejects use of language features⟦P178⟧introduced after the version specified by the ⟦P179⟧ directive. For example, if⟦P180⟧a module has the directive ⟦P181⟧, its packages may not use numeric⟦P182⟧literals like ⟦P183⟧, which were introduced in Go ⟦P184⟧.⟦P185⟧If an older Go version builds one of the module's packages and encounters a⟦P186⟧compile error, the error notes that the module was written for a newer Go⟦P187⟧version. For example, suppose a module has ⟦P188⟧ and a package uses the⟦P189⟧numeric literal ⟦P190⟧. If that package is built with Go ⟦P191⟧, the⟦P192⟧compiler notes that the code is written for Go ⟦P193⟧.⟦P194⟧The ⟦P195⟧ directive also affects the behavior of the ⟦P196⟧ command:⟦P197⟧At ⟦P198⟧ or higher, automatic ⟦P199⟧vendoring⟦P200⟧ may be⟦P201⟧enabled.  If the file ⟦P202⟧ is present and consistent with⟦P203⟧⟦P204⟧, there is no need to explicitly use the ⟦P205⟧ flag.⟦P206⟧At ⟦P207⟧ or higher, the ⟦P208⟧ package pattern matches only packages⟦P209⟧transitively imported by packages and tests in the ⟦P210⟧main⟦P211⟧module⟦P212⟧. This is the same set of packages retained⟦P213⟧by ⟦P214⟧⟦P215⟧⟦P216⟧ since modules were introduced. In⟦P217⟧lower versions, ⟦P218⟧ also includes tests of packages imported by packages in⟦P219⟧the main module, tests of those packages, and so on.⟦P220⟧At ⟦P221⟧ or higher:⟦P222⟧The ⟦P223⟧ file includes an explicit ⟦P224⟧⟦P225⟧⟦P226⟧directive⟦P227⟧ for each module that provides any⟦P228⟧package transitively imported by a package or test in the main module. (At⟦P229⟧⟦P230⟧ and lower, an indirect dependency is included only if ⟦P231⟧minimal⟦P232⟧version selection⟦P233⟧ would otherwise⟦P234⟧select a different version.) This extra information enables ⟦P235⟧module graph⟦P236⟧pruning⟦P237⟧ and ⟦P238⟧lazy module⟦P239⟧loading⟦P240⟧.⟦P241⟧Because there may be many more ⟦P242⟧ dependencies than in previous⟦P243⟧⟦P244⟧ versions, indirect dependencies are recorded in a separate block⟦P245⟧within the ⟦P246⟧ file.⟦P247⟧⟦P248⟧ omits ⟦P249⟧ and ⟦P250⟧ files for vendored⟦P251⟧dependencies. (That allows invocations of the ⟦P252⟧ command within⟦P253⟧subdirectories of ⟦P254⟧ to identify the correct main module.)⟦P255⟧⟦P256⟧ records the ⟦P257⟧ version from each dependency's ⟦P258⟧⟦P259⟧file in ⟦P260⟧.⟦P261⟧At ⟦P262⟧ or higher:⟦P263⟧The ⟦P264⟧ line declares a required minimum version of Go to use with this module.⟦P265⟧The ⟦P266⟧ line must be greater than or equal to the ⟦P267⟧ line of all dependencies.⟦P268⟧The ⟦P269⟧ command no longer attempts to maintain compatibility with the previous older version of Go.⟦P270⟧The ⟦P271⟧ command is more careful about keeping checksums of ⟦P272⟧ files in the ⟦P273⟧ file.⟦P274⟧A ⟦P275⟧ file may contain at most one ⟦P276⟧ directive. Most commands will add a⟦P277⟧⟦P278⟧ directive with the current Go version if one is not present.⟦P279⟧toolchain ⟦P280⟧⟦P281⟧Declares a suggested Go toolchain to use with this module.⟦P282⟧Only takes effect when the module is the main module⟦P283⟧and the default toolchain is older than the suggested toolchain.⟦P284⟧For more see “⟦P285⟧Go toolchains⟦P286⟧” and⟦P287⟧⟦P288⟧⟦P289⟧ directive⟦P290⟧ in the⟦P291⟧Go Modules Reference.⟦P292⟧Syntax ⟦P293⟧⟦P294⟧toolchain-name⟦P295⟧The suggested Go toolchain's name. Standard toolchain names take the form⟦P296⟧⟦P297⟧ for a Go version ⟦P298⟧V⟦P299⟧, as in⟦P300⟧⟦P301⟧ and ⟦P302⟧.⟦P303⟧The special value ⟦P304⟧ disables automatic toolchain switching.⟦P305⟧Examples ⟦P306⟧⟦P307⟧Suggest using Go ⟦P308⟧ or newer:⟦P309⟧Notes ⟦P310⟧⟦P311⟧See “⟦P312⟧Go toolchains⟦P313⟧” for details about how the ⟦P314⟧ line⟦P315⟧affects Go toolchain selection.⟦P316⟧godebug ⟦P317⟧⟦P318⟧Indicates the default ⟦P319⟧⟦P320⟧⟦P321⟧ settings to be applied to the main packages of this module.⟦P322⟧These override any toolchain defaults, and are overridden by explicit ⟦P323⟧ lines in main packages.⟦P324⟧Syntax ⟦P325⟧⟦P326⟧debug-key⟦P327⟧The name of the setting to be applied.⟦P328⟧A list of settings and the versions they were introduced in can be found at⟦P329⟧⟦P330⟧⟦P331⟧ History⟦P332⟧.⟦P333⟧debug-value⟦P334⟧The value provided to the setting.⟦P335⟧If not otherwise specified, ⟦P336⟧ to disable and ⟦P337⟧ to enable the named behavior.⟦P338⟧Examples ⟦P339⟧⟦P340⟧Use the new ⟦P341⟧ ⟦P342⟧ behavior:⟦P343⟧Use the default GODEBUGs from Go ⟦P344⟧, but the old ⟦P345⟧ behavior:⟦P346⟧Notes ⟦P347⟧⟦P348⟧⟦P349⟧ settings only apply for builds of main packages and test binaries in the current module.⟦P350⟧They have no effect when a module is used as a dependency.⟦P351⟧See “⟦P352⟧Go, Backwards Compatibility, and ⟦P353⟧⟦P354⟧” for details on backwards compatibility.⟦P355⟧require ⟦P356⟧⟦P357⟧Declares a module as a dependency of the current module, specifying the⟦P358⟧minimum version of the module required.⟦P359⟧For more, see ⟦P360⟧⟦P361⟧ directive⟦P362⟧ in the⟦P363⟧Go Modules Reference.⟦P364⟧Syntax ⟦P365⟧⟦P366⟧module-path⟦P367⟧The module's module path, usually a concatenation of the module source's⟦P368⟧repository domain and the module name. For module versions v2 and later,⟦P369⟧this value must end with the major version number, such as ⟦P370⟧.⟦P371⟧module-version⟦P372⟧The module's version. This can be either a release version number, such⟦P373⟧as ⟦P374⟧, or a Go-generated pseudo-version number, such as⟦P375⟧⟦P376⟧.⟦P377⟧Examples ⟦P378⟧⟦P379⟧Requiring a released version ⟦P380⟧:⟦P381⟧Requiring a version not yet tagged in its repository by using a pseudo-version⟦P382⟧number generated by Go tools:⟦P383⟧Notes ⟦P384⟧⟦P385⟧When you run a ⟦P386⟧ command such as ⟦P387⟧, Go inserts ⟦P388⟧ directives⟦P389⟧for each module containing imported packages. When a module isn't yet tagged in⟦P390⟧its repository, Go assigns a pseudo-version number it generates when you run the⟦P391⟧command.⟦P392⟧You can have Go require a module from a location other than its repository by⟦P393⟧using the ⟦P394⟧⟦P395⟧ directive⟦P396⟧.⟦P397⟧For more about version numbers, see ⟦P398⟧Module version numbering⟦P399⟧.⟦P400⟧For more about managing dependencies, see the following:⟦P401⟧⟦P402⟧Adding a dependency⟦P403⟧⟦P404⟧⟦P405⟧Getting a specific dependency version⟦P406⟧⟦P407⟧⟦P408⟧Discovering available updates⟦P409⟧⟦P410⟧⟦P411⟧Upgrading or downgrading a dependency⟦P412⟧⟦P413⟧⟦P414⟧Synchronizing your code's dependencies⟦P415⟧⟦P416⟧tool ⟦P417⟧⟦P418⟧Adds a package as a dependency of the current module, and makes it available to run with ⟦P419⟧ when the current working directory is within this module.⟦P420⟧Syntax ⟦P421⟧⟦P422⟧package-path⟦P423⟧The tool's package path, a concatenation of the module containing⟦P424⟧the tool and the (possibly empty) path to the package implementing⟦P425⟧the tool within the module.⟦P426⟧Examples ⟦P427⟧⟦P428⟧Declaring a tool implemented in the current module:⟦P429⟧Declaring a tool implemented in a separate module:⟦P430⟧Notes ⟦P431⟧⟦P432⟧You can use ⟦P433⟧ to run tools declared in your module by fully qualified package path⟦P434⟧or, if there is no ambiguity, by the last path segment. In the first example⟦P435⟧above you could run ⟦P436⟧ or ⟦P437⟧.⟦P438⟧In workspace mode, you can use ⟦P439⟧ to run a tool declared in any workspace module.⟦P440⟧Tools are built using the same module graph as the module itself. A ⟦P441⟧⟦P442⟧⟦P443⟧directive⟦P444⟧ is needed to select the version of the module that⟦P445⟧implements the tool. Any ⟦P446⟧⟦P447⟧ directives⟦P448⟧, or ⟦P449⟧⟦P450⟧⟦P451⟧directives⟦P452⟧ also apply to the tool and its dependencies.⟦P453⟧For more information see ⟦P454⟧Tool dependencies⟦P455⟧.⟦P456⟧replace ⟦P457⟧⟦P458⟧Replaces the content of a module at a specific version (or all versions) with⟦P459⟧another module version or with a local directory. Go tools will use the⟦P460⟧replacement path when resolving the dependency.⟦P461⟧For more, see ⟦P462⟧⟦P463⟧ directive⟦P464⟧ in the⟦P465⟧Go Modules Reference.⟦P466⟧Syntax ⟦P467⟧⟦P468⟧module-path⟦P469⟧The module path of the module to replace.⟦P470⟧module-version⟦P471⟧Optional. A specific version to replace. If this version number is⟦P472⟧omitted, all versions of the module are replaced with the content on the⟦P473⟧right side of the arrow.⟦P474⟧replacement-path⟦P475⟧The path at which Go should look for the required module. This can be a⟦P476⟧module path or a path to a directory on the file system local to the⟦P477⟧replacement module. If this is a module path, you must specify a⟦P478⟧⟦P479⟧replacement-version⟦P480⟧ value. If this is a local path, you may not use a⟦P481⟧⟦P482⟧replacement-version⟦P483⟧ value.⟦P484⟧replacement-version⟦P485⟧The version of the replacement module. The replacement version may only⟦P486⟧be specified if ⟦P487⟧replacement-path⟦P488⟧ is a module path (not a local directory).⟦P489⟧Examples ⟦P490⟧⟦P491⟧Replacing with a fork of the module repository⟦P492⟧In the following example, any version of ⟦P493⟧ is replaced⟦P494⟧with the specified fork of its code.⟦P495⟧When you replace one module path with another, do not change import statements⟦P496⟧for packages in the module you're replacing.⟦P497⟧For more on using a forked copy of module code, see ⟦P498⟧Requiring external module⟦P499⟧code from your own repository fork⟦P500⟧.⟦P501⟧Replacing with a different version number⟦P502⟧The following example specifies that version ⟦P503⟧ should be used instead of⟦P504⟧any other version of the module.⟦P505⟧The following example replaces module version ⟦P506⟧ with version ⟦P507⟧ of⟦P508⟧the same module.⟦P509⟧Replacing with local code⟦P510⟧The following example specifies that a local directory should be used as a⟦P511⟧replacement for all versions of the module.⟦P512⟧The following example specifies that a local directory should be used as a⟦P513⟧replacement for ⟦P514⟧ only.⟦P515⟧For more on using a local copy of module code, see ⟦P516⟧Requiring module code in a⟦P517⟧local directory⟦P518⟧.⟦P519⟧Notes ⟦P520⟧⟦P521⟧Use the ⟦P522⟧ directive to temporarily substitute a module path value with⟦P523⟧another value when you want Go to use the other path to find the module's⟦P524⟧source. This has the effect of redirecting Go's search for the module to the⟦P525⟧replacement's location. You needn't change package import paths to use the⟦P526⟧replacement path.⟦P527⟧Use the ⟦P528⟧ and ⟦P529⟧ directives to control build-time dependency⟦P530⟧resolution when building the current module. These directives are ignored in⟦P531⟧modules that depend on the current module.⟦P532⟧The ⟦P533⟧ directive can be useful in situations such as the following:⟦P534⟧You're developing a new module whose code is not yet in the repository. You⟦P535⟧want to test with clients using a local version.⟦P536⟧You've identified an issue with a dependency, have cloned the dependency's⟦P537⟧repository, and you're testing a fix with the local repository.⟦P538⟧Note that a ⟦P539⟧ directive alone does not add a module to the⟦P540⟧⟦P541⟧module graph⟦P542⟧. A ⟦P543⟧⟦P544⟧ directive⟦P545⟧⟦P546⟧that refers to a replaced module version is also needed, either in the main⟦P547⟧module's ⟦P548⟧ file or a dependency's ⟦P549⟧ file. If you don't have a⟦P550⟧specific version to replace, you can use a fake version, as in the example⟦P551⟧below. Note that this will break modules that depend on your module, since⟦P552⟧⟦P553⟧ directives are only applied in the main module.⟦P554⟧For more on replacing a required module, including using Go tools to make the⟦P555⟧change, see:⟦P556⟧⟦P557⟧Requiring external module code from your own repository⟦P558⟧fork⟦P559⟧⟦P560⟧⟦P561⟧Requiring module code in a local⟦P562⟧directory⟦P563⟧⟦P564⟧For more about version numbers, see ⟦P565⟧Module version⟦P566⟧numbering⟦P567⟧.⟦P568⟧exclude ⟦P569⟧⟦P570⟧Specifies a module or module version to exclude from the current module's⟦P571⟧dependency graph.⟦P572⟧For more, see ⟦P573⟧⟦P574⟧ directive⟦P575⟧ in the⟦P576⟧Go Modules Reference.⟦P577⟧Syntax ⟦P578⟧⟦P579⟧module-path⟦P580⟧The module path of the module to exclude.⟦P581⟧module-version⟦P582⟧The specific version to exclude.⟦P583⟧Example ⟦P584⟧⟦P585⟧Exclude ⟦P586⟧ version ⟦P587⟧⟦P588⟧Notes ⟦P589⟧⟦P590⟧Use the ⟦P591⟧ directive to exclude a specific version of a module that is⟦P592⟧indirectly required but can't be loaded for some reason. For example, you might⟦P593⟧use it to exclude a version of a module that has an invalid checksum.⟦P594⟧Use the ⟦P595⟧ and ⟦P596⟧ directives to control build-time dependency⟦P597⟧resolution when building the current module (the main module you're building).⟦P598⟧These directives are ignored in modules that depend on the current module.⟦P599⟧You can use the ⟦P600⟧⟦P601⟧⟦P602⟧ command⟦P603⟧to exclude a module, as in the following example.⟦P604⟧For more about version numbers, see⟦P605⟧⟦P606⟧Module version numbering⟦P607⟧.⟦P608⟧retract ⟦P609⟧⟦P610⟧Indicates that a version or range of versions of the module defined by ⟦P611⟧⟦P612⟧should not be depended upon. A ⟦P613⟧ directive is useful when a version was⟦P614⟧published prematurely or a severe problem was discovered after the version was⟦P615⟧published.⟦P616⟧For more, see ⟦P617⟧⟦P618⟧ directive⟦P619⟧ in the⟦P620⟧Go Modules Reference.⟦P621⟧Syntax ⟦P622⟧⟦P623⟧version⟦P624⟧A single version to retract.⟦P625⟧version-low⟦P626⟧Lower bound of a range of versions to retract.⟦P627⟧version-high⟦P628⟧Upper bound of a range of versions to retract. Both ⟦P629⟧⟦P630⟧and ⟦P631⟧ are included in the range.⟦P632⟧rationale⟦P633⟧Optional comment explaining the retraction. May be shown in messages to⟦P634⟧the user.⟦P635⟧Example ⟦P636⟧⟦P637⟧Retracting a single version⟦P638⟧Retracting a range of versions⟦P639⟧Notes ⟦P640⟧⟦P641⟧Use the ⟦P642⟧ directive to indicate that a previous version of your module⟦P643⟧should not be used. Users will not automatically upgrade to a retracted version⟦P644⟧with ⟦P645⟧, ⟦P646⟧, or other commands. Users will not see a retracted⟦P647⟧version as an available update with ⟦P648⟧.⟦P649⟧Retracted versions should remain available so users that already depend on them⟦P650⟧are able to build their packages. Even if a retracted version is deleted from⟦P651⟧the source repository, it may remain available on mirrors such as⟦P652⟧⟦P653⟧⟦P654⟧⟦P655⟧. Users that depend on retracted⟦P656⟧versions may be notified when they run ⟦P657⟧ or ⟦P658⟧ on⟦P659⟧related modules.⟦P660⟧The ⟦P661⟧ command discovers retracted versions by reading ⟦P662⟧ directives⟦P663⟧in the ⟦P664⟧ file in the latest version of a module. The latest version is,⟦P665⟧in order of precedence:⟦P666⟧Its highest release version, if any⟦P667⟧Its highest pre-release version, if any⟦P668⟧A pseudo-version for the tip of the repository's default branch.⟦P669⟧When you add a retraction, you almost always need to tag a new, higher version⟦P670⟧so the command will see it in the latest version of the module.⟦P671⟧You can publish a version whose sole purpose is to signal retractions. In this⟦P672⟧case, the new version may also retract itself.⟦P673⟧For example, if you accidentally tag ⟦P674⟧, you can tag ⟦P675⟧ with the⟦P676⟧following directives:⟦P677⟧Unfortunately, once a version is published, it cannot be changed. If you later⟦P678⟧tag ⟦P679⟧ at a different commit, the ⟦P680⟧ command may detect a⟦P681⟧mismatched sum in ⟦P682⟧ or in the ⟦P683⟧checksum⟦P684⟧database⟦P685⟧.⟦P686⟧Retracted versions of a module do not normally appear in the output of⟦P687⟧⟦P688⟧, but you can use the ⟦P689⟧ to show them.⟦P690⟧For more, see ⟦P691⟧⟦P692⟧⟦P693⟧ in the Go Modules Reference.⟦P694⟧ignore ⟦P695⟧⟦P696⟧Specifies directory paths within the module that the ⟦P697⟧ command should⟦P698⟧ignore when matching package patterns.⟦P699⟧For more, see ⟦P700⟧⟦P701⟧ directive⟦P702⟧ in the⟦P703⟧Go Modules Reference.⟦P704⟧Syntax ⟦P705⟧⟦P706⟧path⟦P707⟧A slash-separated path to ignore. If the path starts with ⟦P708⟧,⟦P709⟧it is interpreted relative to the module root directory. Otherwise, any⟦P710⟧directory with that name at any depth within the module is ignored.⟦P711⟧Examples ⟦P712⟧⟦P713⟧Ignore a local directory relative to the module root⟦P714⟧Ignore all directories named ⟦P715⟧ anywhere in the module⟦P716⟧Ignore multiple paths using a block⟦P717⟧Notes ⟦P718⟧⟦P719⟧Use the ⟦P720⟧ directive to prevent the ⟦P721⟧ command from matching⟦P722⟧packages in generated or non-Go directories when using⟦P723⟧wildcard patterns like ⟦P724⟧. Ignored directories and their contents⟦P725⟧are excluded from package patterns but are still present in the module⟦P726⟧file tree.⟦P727⟧The ⟦P728⟧ directive only applies in the main module's ⟦P729⟧ file.⟦P730⟧It has no effect in ⟦P731⟧ files of dependencies.⟦P732⟧
